<BaseLayout title="About PARADOX" description="What PARADOX is, how static analysis works, and what VERIFIED does and does not mean." canonical="https://paradox.engineer/about/">
  <main class="legal">
    <p class="label">ABOUT</p>
    <h1>AI agent intelligence, not another chatbot.</h1>
    <article>
      <p>PARADOX helps developers and AI builders discover public AI agents, skills and GitHub projects, then inspect whether those projects look active, documented and potentially risky — before installing them.</p>
      <h2>How analysis works</h2>
      <p>The verifier reads public GitHub metadata and a limited set of files (README, manifests, Docker files, workflows). It runs <strong>deterministic static analysis</strong> in the browser or, when configured, in a Supabase Edge Function. Repository contents are treated as data. Instructions inside READMEs are never followed.</p>
      <h2>What VERIFIED means</h2>
      <p>VERIFIED means the repository is public, analyzable, has usable documentation, and did not cluster high static-risk patterns. It does <strong>not</strong> mean the software is secure, reviewed, or safe to run.</p>
      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>We do not execute cloned code, Dockerfiles, or install scripts. We do not certify security. Evidence can be incomplete, stale the moment a commit lands, or miss obfuscated behavior. Unknown is shown when we lack evidence.</p>
      <h2>Source methodology</h2>
      <p>Discovery uses a curated catalog plus GitHub Search. Trending uses recent GitHub activity and catalog inclusion reasons — never claimed Google search volume.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
